1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:09,840
Um, when we look at the reference architecture, uh, there's this focus on generating a semantic map, and establishing that meaning is actually the hardest part of the four

2
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:11,020
interoperability layers.

3
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:15,440
Yeah. Exactly. It's, um, it's slow, it's unglamorous,

4
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:20,260
and it is highly political, which is why projects often just skip it entirely.

5
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:21,400
Right, they just bypass it.

6
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:26,780
So what are the consequences in your ministry if this semantic layer is skipped?

7
00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:34,380
Well, exchanges silently fail. I mean, a working wire that carries misread data is actually a much worse failure because,

8
00:00:34,820 --> 00:00:36,600
you know, it looks exactly like success.

9
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:39,740
Oh, wow, right, because the connection technically worked.

10
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:45,460
Exactly. The, the only real solution is building a semantic map to make that meaning tractable.

11
00:00:46,140 --> 00:00:50,920
And, uh, what's interesting now is that AI can actually draft the first version of that map.

12
00:00:51,580 --> 00:00:58,400
Okay, so if we're building this map, what exactly does a semantic map set out for, uh, a single data exchange?

13
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:01,460
So it sets out five things in this order.

14
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:05,780
Number one, the entities. Like, are we talking about a learner or a school?

15
00:01:05,980 --> 00:01:06,900
Right, the core subject.

16
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:07,300
Yeah.

17
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:11,920
Number two, their fields, so name, date of birth, enrollment status.

18
00:01:12,140 --> 00:01:20,100
Got it. Number three, and this is often the tricky part, the code lists, because, you know, the allowed values really matter.

19
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:23,700
One agency's active is another agency's enrolled.

20
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:26,560
Ah, so they need to establish a shared vocabulary there.

21
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:29,380
Right. Then number four is the identifier.

22
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:33,460
That is the key linking the exact same learner across two different agencies.

23
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:40,660
And finally, number five is the mapping itself, which reconciles each agency's terms into one shared definition.

24
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:46,340
Okay, but doing all that mapping, um, does your program have to invent these definitions from scratch?

25
00:01:46,740 --> 00:01:48,160
No. Thankfully, no.

26
00:01:48,780 --> 00:01:50,960
I mean, much of the meaning is already published out there.

27
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:52,600
Wait, really? Like what?

28
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:53,200
Well,

29
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:58,980
you use one roster for enrollment data, uh, CEDS for common education vocabulary,

30
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:04,980
and ISO IEC 11179 to describe elements precisely.

31
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:06,460
Okay, that makes sense.

32
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:13,260
And for credentials, you'd use W3C verifiable credentials, so you definitely do not start from a blank page.

33
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:16,940
You just map your agency's fields onto these published standards.

34
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:23,980
That's a huge head start. But, um, is deciding what enrollment means a technical choice for the IT team?

35
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:27,340
No, it is absolutely not a technical choice.

36
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:30,260
It is a business decision made by the data owners.

37
00:02:30,580 --> 00:02:32,540
Wait, the data owners, not IT.

38
00:02:33,300 --> 00:02:37,120
Exactly. If systems become siloed or costly to change,

39
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:40,460
it's often because this wasn't agreed upon early on.

40
00:02:41,380 --> 00:02:46,520
The semantic map acts as the shared language where data owners and IT architects agree just once.

41
00:02:47,420 --> 00:02:48,920
Which avoids duplicated effort, right?

42
00:02:49,420 --> 00:02:54,880
Yes. A- And it stops them from constantly rearguing definitions every single time they build something new.

43
00:02:55,720 --> 00:03:00,840
So going back to the AI part, uh, how do we safely use AI to draft this mapping?

44
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:05,440
Well, you give Claude the published vocabulary and the two agencies' field lists.

45
00:03:06,180 --> 00:03:10,380
It drafts the 90%, so the mappings, the translations, the identifiers.

46
00:03:10,880 --> 00:03:11,960
Ooh, that saves a ton of time.

47
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:16,760
It does, but, um, however, that essential 10% is yours.

48
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:21,380
You must manually confirm every identifier and code value against real registries.

49
00:03:21,860 --> 00:03:23,580
Because if the AI gets it wrong...

50
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:28,060
An invented mapping reads incredibly convincingly, but it's wrong,

51
00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:29,800
and wrong here

52
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:34,360
means a learner's records are silently merged with someone else's.

53
00:03:34,380 --> 00:03:37,120
Ugh. Yikes. Yeah. It's a huge issue.

54
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:41,720
So the rule is generate fast, confirm carefully.

55
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:49,560
You make the hardest layer tractable by building a semantic map, defining those entities, fields, code lists, the identifier,

56
00:03:49,980 --> 00:03:51,820
and the mapping one exchange at a time.

57
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:56,640
It is the shared language, AI generated and human confirmed.

58
00:03:57,480 --> 00:04:00,900
Getting it right ensures the exchange carries meaning, not just bytes.

59
00:04:01,660 --> 00:04:04,260
That is the difference between interoperability that works

60
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:07,400
and interoperability that only appears to.

61
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:09,480
Sources are in the video description.
